{"reply":"In Chapter 8, the gardeners explain that the roses were planted incorrectly and that they are repainting them because, “if the Queen was to find it out, we should all have our heads cut off.” When the Queen arrives, they immediately flatten themselves. The scene shows concealment organized around anticipated punishment; within this passage, it does not establish that the Queen discovers the mistake.

Your note has a different surface: you found that the billing mistake was yours, fixed it, and then said nothing. The shared question is whether correction ends the matter—or whether the error still needs to be made visible. A 2023 study of hospital nurses found that leader inclusiveness, safety climate, and psychological safety significantly affected willingness to report errors, and describes reporting as an interpersonal risk ([Munn et al., 2023](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)). That research offers a possible lens, not an explanation of your motive.

